UID:NjjQTSHNHs
DESCRIPTION:Duration: 101 mins (1h41m)\n\nFilm essayist and SIFF alum Alex
 andre O. Philippe (78/52\, Lynch/Oz) calls upon five horror luminaries—S
 tephen King and Takashi Miike among them—to discuss the impact 1974’s 
 seminal The Texas Chain Saw Massacre had upon their lives and careers. Di
 rector Alexandre Philippe scheduled to attend. Swap your leather face for 
 your thinking cap at the May 16 screening\, where BoneBat Comedy of Horror
 s Film Fest will be giving away goodies via trivia! Sponsored by:\n\nhttps
 ://www.siff.net/festival/chain-reactions\n\nAlso playing at:\n  • SIFF C
 inema Uptown House 2 (Fri 16 May\, 9:00 PM)
LOCATION:AMC Pacific Place 11\, 600 Pine Street
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Librarians
DTSTART:20250517T210000Z
DTEND:20250517T224000Z
DTSTAMP:20250517T210000Z
UID:Nr1gXUfM4D
DESCRIPTION:Duration: 88 mins (1h28m)\n\nWarriors come in all shapes and s
 izes\, as America’s librarians fight tooth and nail to protect us from t
 he pervasive conservative censorship that threatens to eliminate free thou
 ght in the latest from Oscar®-nominated director Kim A. Snyder (Death by 
 Numbers). Director Kim A. Snyder and librarian Gavin Downing scheduled to 
 attend. Sponsored by:\n\nhttps://www.siff.net/festival/the-librarians\n\nA
 lso playing at:\n  • SIFF Cinema Uptown House 2 (Fri 16 May\, 6:30 PM)
LOCATION:SIFF Cinema Uptown House 2\, 511 Queen Anne Avenue North
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Billy
DTSTART:20250517T213000Z
DTEND:20250517T233000Z
DTSTAMP:20250517T213000Z
UID:n148Jf985T
DESCRIPTION:Duration: 107 mins (1h47m)\n\nIn this compassionate yet distur
 bing documentary\, director Lawrence Côté-Collins inspects the life of f
 ormer filmmaking friend Billy Poulin\, now in prison for murder\, through 
 his own stockpile of video footage and deconstructs the effects of untreat
 ed schizophrenia. Sponsored by:\n\nhttps://www.siff.net/festival/billy\n\n
 Also playing at:\n  • SIFF Film Center (Fri 16 May\, 5:30 PM)\n\nFilm is
  also being streamed.
LOCATION:SIFF Film Center\, 305 Harrison Street
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Remaining Native
DTSTART:20250517T213000Z
DTEND:20250517T231000Z
DTSTAMP:20250517T213000Z
UID:lghcxFwU0Y
DESCRIPTION:Duration: 87 mins (1h27m)\n\nAs Indigenous teenager Ku Stevens
  aspires to go to the University of Oregon and become an Olympic-level cro
 ss-country runner\, we learn of his family’s history through his great-g
 randfather’s narrow escape from the horrible clutches of an Indian board
 ing school. Director Paige Bethmann\, producer Jessica Epstein\, subject K
 u Stevens\, and executive producer Billy Mills (Olympic Gold Medalist) sch
 eduled to attend. Sponsored by:\n\nhttps://www.siff.net/festival/remaining
 -native\n\nAlso playing at:\n  • SIFF Cinema Uptown House 2 (Sun 18 May\
 , 5:00 PM)\n\nFilm is also being streamed.
LOCATION:SIFF Cinema Uptown House 1\, 511 Queen Anne Avenue North
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Viktor
DTSTART:20250517T213000Z
DTEND:20250517T231000Z
DTSTAMP:20250517T213000Z
UID:UDl5OCov7T
DESCRIPTION:Duration: 89 mins (1h29m)\n\nWhen Russia invaded Ukraine\, Vik
 tor Korotovskyi rallied to join the military but was rejected due to his d
 eafness. Instead\, he became a war photographer. Join Viktor on the frontl
 ines in this wholly immersive and terrifying documentary from producer Dar
 ren Aronofsky. Attend the May 17 screening for a chance to win a book of s
 tunning Ukrainian photography\, courtesy of Ukraine Defense Support. Spons
 ored by:\n\nhttps://www.siff.net/festival/viktor\n\nAlso playing at:\n  
 • SIFF Cinema Downtown (Mon 19 May\, 9:30 PM)\n\nFilm is also being stre
 amed.
LOCATION:SIFF Cinema Downtown\, 2100 4th Avenue
END:VEVENT
